\title{Coherent Transport of Anyons in a Triple-Antidot System via Wigner-Jordan Description}

\begin{abstract}
We theoretically investigate the coherent transport of abelian anyons through a linear array of three quantum Hall antidots at fractional filling factors $\nu = 1/(2m+1)$ with $m \in \mathbb{N}_0$. To properly account for the non-trivial exchange statistics, we employ an extended one-dimensional Wigner-Jordan transformation that maps the localized fractional quasiparticle operators onto an effective lattice model incorporating non-local statistical string phases. We calculate the coherent transmission probability and current-voltage characteristics as a function of magnetic flux, local gate potentials, and inter-antidot coupling strengths. Our analysis reveals that the statistical parameter $g = 1/(2m+1)$ significantly alters the resonant tunneling spectrum, generating asymmetric transmission peaks and modifying the conditions for coherent destruction of tunneling (dark states) compared to standard electronic or bosonic triple-dot setups. Furthermore, we show that the interplay between intra-antidot Coulomb interactions and the non-local Wigner-Jordan phase gives rise to fractional Aharonov-Bohm oscillations with statistical-dependent phase shifts. This work provides clear, experimentally accessible transport signatures to isolate and verify anyonic coherence in multi-antidot interferometers.
\end{abstract}

